Essay seed: fragile-per-session vs self-healing-shared — a design principle surfaced by the aicli title-keeper (2026-08-01).

The original mechanism (reassert_title_loop) was a background child of ONE session's shell. The code was correct — a fresh launch worked. The FLAW was structural: a per-session helper that dies (system wedge, stray signal) never recovers, and nothing notices. Observed live: after an autofs/mount hang froze stat/xdotool-adjacent work, several long-running sessions silently lost their title-keepers; their window titles drifted permanently while freshly-launched ones still worked. So the bug wasn't 'it doesn't work', it was 'it stops working invisibly and stays broken.'

The fix wasn't 'restart the loop when it dies' (still per-session, still fragile) — it was to change the SHAPE: one shared, self-healing supervisor decoupled from any single session, reading durable per-session facts (.wid/.title) from disk and re-asserting for the whole live set each tick. Individual session death can't stop it; it reconstructs desired state from disk rather than holding it in a fragile in-process loop.

The general principle for the essay: when a mechanism's failure is SILENT and UNRECOVERABLE, prefer generalising it into shared infrastructure that rebuilds desired state from durable facts, over patching the per-instance helper to be more robust. 'Special cases layered on shared infra are a sign the fix isn't deep enough.' The state-from-disk reconstruction is also why it's robust — same reason STATE.md (not the transcript) is the strand's memory.
